  [ref] Most common winning margins, 2016–2025: 14.4% of games are decided by exactly 3 and 8.5% by 7. Half a point is worth the most around those two numbers.
  [ref] Push rate when the spread sits on a key number: A spread of exactly 3 pushes 10.1% of the time; a spread of 7, 5.5%.
  [ref] Most common final totals: Totals do not cluster. The most common final total, 51, is 4.0% of games.
  [thin] Six-point teaser legs by spread, 2016–2025: Underdogs of 1 to 2.5 teased up six win 76.2% of legs against a 73.9% bar. Favorites clear it only at a spread of exactly 7. Books that charge more than 1.83 for two teams erase the edge.
  [bad] Six-point teaser legs on totals: No total, teased either way, wins more than 70.3% of legs. The bar is 73.9%.
  [bad] Moneylines by spread size, 2016–2025: Underdogs of 10 or more return -21.9% and -15.1% on the moneyline. The smallest underdogs (1 to 2.5) lose the least, -1.3%.
  [ref] 2026 luck by team: DEN has won 2.2 more games than its points say and TB 2.2 fewer. Both should drift back toward their point differential.
  [priced] Does luck reverse? Weeks 1–9 against the rest of the season, 2016–2025: Lucky teams do fall back, from winning 72.2% of games to 55.7%, but they cover 48.7% afterwards. The spread sees it coming.
  [ref] Home-field advantage by season: Home teams won by 2.07 points a game in 2025 and the market priced 1.5. Home field is worth about two points now, not three.
  [noise] Home-field advantage by team, 2021–2026: The largest measured home edge is CHI at 4.6 points, on about 45 home games. Too few to adjust a line by.
  [ref] 2026 scoring by quarter, per game: JAX has the best first-half margin (+10.5 a game) and TEN the worst (-7.9). Four games each.
  [ref] 2026 decisions and finishing: The league goes for it on 69% of fourth-and-shorts and scores a touchdown on 63% of red-zone trips.
  [noise] Current head coaches against the spread, 2016–2026: Among coaches with 150+ games, the best is Sean Payton at 58.1% against the spread. Everyone above that on the list has a short track record.
  [noise] Each team's most recent starting quarterback, 2016–2026: Among quarterbacks with 100+ starts, the best is Jared Goff at 57.8% against the spread. Shorter records above him are small samples.
  [priced] A quarterback's first three career starts (since 2017): A quarterback in his first three starts wins only 34.1% of the time but covers 49.5%. The spread already accounts for him.
  [noise] Referees working in 2026: results in their games, 2016–2026: Referees with 100+ games range from 42.4% to 52.0% overs, which is about what chance alone produces.
  [noise] Do flag-happy crews change totals?: Crews that throw the most flags: 48.8% overs. The fewest: 47.7%. Penalties do not move totals.
  [noise] Situational records: All 12 spots land between 48.1% and 52.7% against the spread over ten years. None is worth a bet by itself.
  [holds] Wind, outdoor games: Unders hit 57.9% in outdoor games with 10 to 19 mph wind (525 games). Wind is the one weather effect the total has not fully absorbed.
  [thin] Time slot: Sunday night and Monday night unders: 56.3% over 359 games. Every other slot is a coin flip.
  [priced] Playing surface, 2016–2025: Turf games score more (46.9 points against 44.6 on grass) and the total is already set higher.
  [priced] Temperature, outdoor games: Freezing games score 41.3 points against a total of 43.0, but that is 88 games. The total already drops with the temperature.
  [ref] How often each role scores a touchdown, by the team's implied points, 2016–2025: A lead back scores in 28.3% of games when his team is implied under 17.5 points and 56.7% at 27 or more. A top receiver: 21.6% and 41.0%.
  [ref] Reaching 100 yards, by season average coming in: A back averaging 85 to 99 rushing yards reaches 100 in 31.0% of games. A season average is nearer a coin flip than a floor.
